INT. ALL-NIGHT LAUNDROMAT - 3 A.M.

Fluorescent tubes buzz. Rows of orange plastic chairs, a broken change machine with a handwritten sign, a wall of dryers like portholes. Rain on the glass.

MARISOL REYES (17, hoodie, backpack stuffed with a prom dress in a garment bag) sits on a washer, knees up. TEDDY OYELARAN (17, tall, thin, a cast on his left wrist) feeds quarters into a dryer. NKECHI BELL (16, Marisol's cousin, braids, school jacket) paces. CAL DRUMMOND (18, ex-boyfriend, letterman jacket, car keys in his fist) stands by the door, dripping.

CAL
Everybody just calm down.

NKECHI
(to Marisol)
You told me he wasn't coming.

MARISOL
I didn't know.

CAL
I just want to talk.

TEDDY
(muttering)
Here we go.

MARISOL
(to the room)
Okay, nobody is doing this tonight.

Cal steps forward. Teddy puts down the quarters and stands between them, wrist cast raised like a stop sign.

TEDDY
Cal. Buddy. It's three in the morning.

CAL
Stay out of it, Teddy.

TEDDY
I'm in it. I'm holding her dress.

He is. He doesn't remember picking it up.

NKECHI
(to herself)
I'm calling my mom.

MARISOL
Don't you dare.

Cal's fist closes on the keys. Then, slowly, he opens it. He lets the keys drop onto the chair and sits down hard, shoulders loose.

CAL
I drove forty minutes. I don't even know what I was going to say.

Nobody answers. A dryer buzzes. Marisol slides off the washer.

MARISOL
Then say it. You've got until this cycle's over.

She sets the timer on the dryer. Thirty-one minutes. Teddy hands Cal a warm towel from the load without a word.

NKECHI
(to Teddy)
That's somebody's towel.

TEDDY
It's everybody's now.
